[00:00.84] (The Great Dust Storm)
[00:07.16] On the 14th day of April of 1935,
[00:19.60] There struck the worst of dust storms that ever filled the sky.
[00:54.64] You could see that dust storm comin', the cloud looked deathlike black,
[01:25.76] And through our mighty nation, it left a dreadful track.
[01:26.26] From Oklahoma City to the Arizona line,
[01:26.26] Dakota and Nebraska to the lazy Rio Grande,
[01:26.26] It fell across our city like a curtain of black rolled down,
[01:29.98] We thought it was our judgement, we thought it was our doom.
[01:53.72] The radio reported, we listened with alarm,
[01:53.76] The wild and windy actions of this great mysterious storm;
[01:53.76] From Albuquerque and Clovis, and all New Mexico,
[01:53.76] They said it was the blackest that ever they had saw.
[01:53.80] From old Dodge City, Kansas, the dust had rung their knell,
[01:55.56] And a few more comrades sleeping on top of old Boot Hill.
[01:55.94] From Denver, Colorado, they said it blew so strong,
[02:00.62] They thought that they could hold out, but they didn't know how long.
[02:13.60] Our relatives were huddled into their oil boom shacks,
[02:13.60] And the children they was cryin' as it whistled through the cracks.
[02:14.74] And the family it was crowded into their little room,
[02:15.56] They thought the world had ended, and they thought it was their doom.
[02:20.78] The storm took place at sundown, it lasted through the night,
[02:32.70] When we looked out next morning, we saw a terrible sight.
[02:35.42] We saw outside our window where wheat fields they had grown
[02:35.42] Was now a rippling ocean of dust the wind had blown.
[02:35.42] It covered up our fences, it covered up our barns,
[02:35.42] It covered up our tractors in this wild and dusty storm.
[02:40.70] We loaded our jalopies and piled our families in,
[02:45.40] We rattled down that highway to never come back again.
